al Obstruction", "level": 1, "posterior": 0.6711548420507509, "danger": 0.6, "evidence_for": ["A prior surgical scar combined with tympanic abdomen suggests adhesion-related mechanical obstruction, but lacks definitive imaging confirmation. This provides weak contextual support for B1.", "Diabetic gastroparesis aligns with postprandial symptom exacerbation, though mechanical obstruction remains more likely given physical exam. This provides moderate support for B5.", "Physical findings of distension and scar suggest mechanical rather than functional causes, but diabetes-related neuropathy keeps B3 viable. This weakens B3.", "Vague symptoms without biochemical evidence reduce confidence in metabolic causes, but diabetes history still provides moderate support. This yields neutral for B5.", "The findings of a distended abdomen with a right lower quadrant scar and meal-triggered symptoms provide weak to moderate support for mechanical obstruction (B1), while diabetes-related autonomic neuropathy risk and absence of confirmatory testing slightly reduce support for neurogenic/functional disorders (B3) and metabolic/motility disorders (B5). The evidence remains insufficient to definitively exclude other live branches.", "The scar and distension suggest prior abdominal surgery, which increases the risk of adhesions or mechanical obstruction. This provides weak support for B1."], "evidence_against": ["While norovirus is common in cruise-related outbreaks, the absence of fever spikes and dehydration weakens this association. This yields neutral impact on B2.", "Diabetic autonomic neuropathy explains delayed gastric emptying consistent with functional disorders, but the physical exam findings (distension, scar) suggest mechanical causes. This provides weak support for B3.", "Subacute presentation is inconsistent with acute mechanical obstruction, but the physical exam findings (distension, weak pulses) maintain mechanical obstruction as a leading hypothesis. This weakens B1 s